reviewer_comments.reviewer_2:
high performance p-type transistors is fundamentally crucial for atomically monolithic thin TMDs-based next beyond-silicon electronics via the common CMOS technology. This manuscript demonstrates a novel epitaxial growth strategy of metallic-phase tellurium (m-Te) on MoTe2 via evaporation as an efficient p-type contact. Through the comprehensive investigation, monoclinic metallic P21 Te which typically requires high-pressure synthesis can be obtained through simple evaporation with the (021) plane aligned to the MoTe2 (100) plane. Through this novel contact architecture, the authors claim that metallic-Te is feasible for the efficient p-type contact of MoTe₂ transistors with near-barrier-free hole injection. Ultimately, the authors benchmark the bilayer MoTe2 transistor performance with the current reports. We consider the epitaxial growth of m-Te on MoTe2 (100) plane was systematically investigated and such strategy offers another process selection to achieve good p-type contact quality of MoTe2 transistors. However, some crucial concerns of the actual feasibility of m-Te as a p-type contact for MoTe2 transistors should be addressed before its consideration for the publication in Nature Communications as followed:
1.The authors have performed systematic investigation of vdW epitaxy of m-Te on MoTe2, and the results are impressive and intriguing. In this section, the first concern is whether chemical bonding occurs during the m-Te deposition on MoTe2 film, which requires more experimental verification such as XPS characterization. The vdW epitaxial growth is quite important for the contact quality and the following discussion. Secondly, as the authors claimed, the elemental Te generally exhibit a semiconducting trigonal phase under ambient conditions. We wonder except for the lattice match consideration, whether deposition conditions such as temperature would impact the m-Te phase formation. Whether the authors have verified how to control and optimize the M-Te phase formation? In other words, whether semiconducting Te film can be grown in MoTe2 nanosheets and/or the substrate such as SiO2/Si in other area? Thirdly, how to verify the possible defects introduction such as the damage to the MoTe2 channel during the deposition process, which is commonly unavoidable for traditional metal thermal deposition? Ultimately, the crucial concern in this section relies in the insufficient information about the m-Te film. Although the authors show the crystal structure and XRD pattern of m-Te film, however, its vital material characteristics for suitable contact electrodes should be further clarified. For example, the resistivity-temperature relationship should be given to verify its metallic nature except for the electronic structure theoretical calculation. The crucial factor of work function should be also verified by UPS, and the band alignment between m-Te and MoTe2 should be also verified by KPFM characterization;
2.The authors have claimed that the m-Te/MoTe2 interface enables barrier-free hole injection, however, such claim is lack of crucial evidences. Firstly, in line 205-211, the work function of m-Te was determined as 4.65 eV, which is lower than the VBM of MoTe2. Such DFT theoretical calculation requires further experimental verification. We understand that these values are easily underestimated by theoretical calculation, however, the VBM of MoTe2 has been well verified as ~4.8-5.0 eV experimentally. In this regard, the direct band alignment should be supplemented. Secondly, In Fig. 3b, the MIGS should be given with the magnified PLDOS in the bandgap. We doubted what is the special features of m-Te can indeed alleviate the MIGS, which enables the m-Te differing from other contact metal electrodes. The authors should clarify that. Thirdly, for the electrical performance of the contacts, more crucial evidences should be supplemented. For example, in Fig. 4b, although the m-Te/MoTe2 contact shows linear output characteristics in room temperature, the similar output characteristics at low temperature should be also clarified, since thermionic emission was suppressed at low temperature. Meanwhile, the linear characteristics in Fig. 4b is reasonable in the range of 0V to -20V, because the device is always operated in “on” state in this gate range from Fig 4a. In this regard, the gate voltage should be given from higher than 20V to -20V. Similarly and vitally, the authors plotted an Arrhenius curve in Fig. 4d and claimed that positive slopes across different gate voltages thus providing direct evidence for barrier-free charge injection. However, the device is always operated in “on” state in this gate voltage range from -10V to -25V, implying the bend bending of MoTe2 channel and barrier-free for hole injection. The authors should provide the extracted barrier height at different gate voltage, thus to obtain the barrier height at flat-band. Another direct experimental evidence for the barrier-free hole injection lies in the increased on-state current with the decreased temperature, and the Ids-Vds curves as function of temperature should be also clarified;
3.Ultimately, the authors benchmark their devices with the state-of-the-art 2D p-type FETs based on MoTe2 (blue) and undoped WSe2 (orange) (Figs. 4g, h). Firstly, to our best known, p-type WSe₂ transistors have already achieved Rc as low as 0.041 kΩ·μm (Science 388, 1183–1188 (2025)) and on-current up to 1.5 mA/μm (Nat. Electron. 5, 497–504 (2022)). The comparison with WSe₂ should be more cautious. Meanwhile, recent investigations such as NO surface doping (Nat. Commun. 16, 4160 (2025)), semimetal contacts (ACS Appl. Mater. Interfaces 17, 25518–25525 (2025); Nat. Commun. 14, 4747 (2023)) etc. have also shown excellent p-type electronic performance. The authors should also benchmark these investigations;
4.Some more experimental details should be also supplemented. For example, for device fabrication, the exfoliated MoTe2 nanosheets were adopted as the device channel. In such case, whether a large-scale M-Te film can be obtained? how to etch the unnecessary Te film selectively from the MoTe2 nanosheets and underlying substrate? How the etching process impact the quality of MoTe2 channel? Whether preferable growth of M-Te on the contact area is feasible? In Fig. 4, bilayer MoTe₂ transistors were investigated. Further supporting evidence such as Raman, AFM step height, or optical contrast calibration should be provided to verify its bilayer thickness. Meanwhile, for the device electronic characteristics, the SS values should be also given to further verify the contact quality and high-k dielectric/channel quality.
